From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 19:05:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] The depth image cannot answer for a depthless surface

Patch 0016 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user's Hits view settled this, where five readings of the code had not. In that view green is
a kept hit and red is a hit the cut-out depth compare dropped. On the rung without the alpha test
the artifact is a large solid RED triangle. With the alpha test it becomes red tree SHAPED
speckle: the see-through texels had stopped registering, and the leaves that remained were still
being dropped.

So the triangles are that compare. It drops a traced hit lying in front of what the rasterized
depth image shows, on the reasoning that such a hit is the see-through part of an alpha tested
quad, and that reasoning assumes the surface is in the image. This game's foliage is not: it reads
depth and never writes it, so at a pixel showing a leaf the image still holds the wall behind, and
the ray is sent on to the wall. The leaf is then lit as the wall is, and every disagreement
between the two shows as the quad's own shape.

THIS WAS TRIED ONCE AND REVERTED, and the difference is the whole point. That attempt removed the
compare while the alpha test was not running on these draws, so every transparent texel became
surface, which was worse than the fault. The compare was the only thing dropping them. With the
alpha test in place they are dropped first and on their own merit, so the compare has nothing left
to catch here. Both halves at once is what neither attempt had.

Also retires the two rungs that raised the triangle count. They were meant to keep a fairy's glow
out of the classification and they threw the trees out with it: an OoT pine's foliage is a couple
of crossed quads, which is four triangles. A triangle count cannot separate a sprite from a tree
in this game because they are the same size. Their places are kept so the row does not renumber
under somebody mid-test.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -58,6 +58,11 @@ struct ShadowPayload {
 
 // The alpha test at the hit (below the passes): how much of the pixel a surface covers.
 float alphaCoverage(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex, float2 barycentrics, bool primary);
+// Whether the surface a hit landed on is one the depth image can be asked about (patch 0016):
+// false for a draw in the translucent z mode that never writes depth, which is this game's
+// foliage. Defined beside alphaCoverage because it reads the same two buffers, and declared here
+// because the primary ray's cut-out compare needs it long before those buffers exist.
+bool hitWritesDepth(int instanceId);
 
 // The eye, from the inverse view matrix. The parameter block is written by hlslpp on the CPU
 // with row vectors, so its translation is the fourth row there; HLSL packs the same bytes as a
@@ -201,6 +206,25 @@ void PrimaryRayGen() {
             if (rasterDepth <= hitDepth + CutoutDepthSlack) {
                 break;
             }
+            // A SURFACE THAT NEVER WRITES DEPTH IS NOT IN THE DEPTH IMAGE (patch 0016), so asking
+            // this image about it gets an answer about whatever is BEHIND it. This game's foliage
+            // is exactly that, and at a pixel showing a leaf the image still holds the wall: the
+            // compare sees a hit far in front of the picture, drops it, and the ray walks on to
+            // the wall, so the leaf is lit as the wall is. That is the triangle.
+            //
+            // HIS HITS VIEW IS WHAT PROVED IT rather than any reading of mine: on the rung without
+            // the alpha test the artifact is a solid RED triangle, red being a hit this compare
+            // dropped; with the alpha test it becomes red tree-SHAPED speckle, so the see-through
+            // texels had stopped registering and the remaining LEAVES were still being dropped.
+            //
+            // THIS WAS TRIED ONCE AND REVERTED, and the difference now is the whole point. That
+            // attempt removed the compare while the alpha test was NOT running on these draws, so
+            // every transparent texel became surface and it was worse than the fault. The compare
+            // was the only thing dropping them. Now the alpha test drops them first and on their
+            // own merit, so the compare has nothing left to catch here.
+            if ((RtParams.tuneExperiment.x >= 24.0f) && !hitWritesDepth(payload.instanceId)) {
+                break;
+            }
             depthsWeighed = float2(rasterDepth, hitDepth);
             if (step == 3) {
                 // Nothing along the ray agreed with the picture: a miss.
@@ -1308,6 +1332,21 @@ void SurfaceClosestHit(inout SurfacePayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersect
 // pixel and always tests the texel.
 // Returns how much of the pixel the surface covers here: 1 for a solid draw or a covered texel,
 // 0 for a cut one, and a fraction far away where several texels fall in the pixel (below).
+bool hitWritesDepth(int instanceId) {
+    if (instanceId < 0) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[instanceId];
+    const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
+    const OtherMode otherMode = { rp.omL, rp.omH };
+    // Narrowed to the class this is about, so a change meant for foliage cannot quietly alter a
+    // decal or a depthless opaque draw somewhere else in the game.
+    if ((otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !otherMode.zUpd()) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    return true;
+}
+
 float alphaCoverage(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex, float2 barycentrics, bool primary) {
     const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[instanceId];
     const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1898,8 +1898,22 @@ namespace RT64 {
                             // Bracketed rather than guessed at one number, because the right cut
                             // sits somewhere between a sprite and a tree and neither of us knows
                             // where: a pine is a few crossed quads, a hedge line is many.
-                            case 22: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && (call.callDesc.triangleCount > 4); castsNothing = true; break;
-                            case 23: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && (call.callDesc.triangleCount > 16); castsNothing = true; break;
+                            // 22 and 23 RETIRED (patch 0016). They raised the bar to four and
+                            // sixteen triangles to keep a fairy's glow out of the classification,
+                            // and they threw the TREES out with it: an OoT pine's foliage is a
+                            // couple of crossed quads, which is four triangles. A triangle count
+                            // cannot separate a sprite from a tree in this game because they are
+                            // the same size. Left in place so the row's numbering does not shift
+                            // under a person mid-test, and doing nothing but the winning rule.
+                            case 22:
+                            case 23: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; castsNothing = true; break;
+                            // 24 and 25 are rung 20 AND the cut-out depth compare skipped for
+                            // these draws (patch 0016, in the raygen). Both halves at once, which
+                            // is what neither earlier attempt had: the alpha test drops the
+                            // see-through texels, and the compare stops dropping the leaves that
+                            // survive it. 24 keeps the shadow off, 25 puts it back.
+                            case 24: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; castsNothing = true; break;
+                            case 25: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
                             default: solidDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER); break;
                         }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
